From c605e876f4312a4461d006c5ea70276c2d48e621 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Struan Bartlett <struan.bartlett@NewsNow.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:07:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] passthrough: Let the guest decide on setuid/setgid in
 copy_file_range

With KILLPRIV_V2, the guest kernel leaves clearing the setuid and
setgid bits of a modified file to virtiofsd, and says when to with a
flag on open, write and setattr, made from the caller's capabilities:
a caller with CAP_FSETID keeps the bits, as on a local file system.
copy_file_range carries no such flag, so we clear the bits for every
caller, including privileged ones, e.g. root's 'cp' over an existing
setuid file, which uses copy_file_range.

Where the destination has setuid, or setgid with group-execute, bits,
decline the copy with EOPNOTSUPP. The guest kernel's FUSE client then
falls back to copying with ordinary writes (as Linux 5.15, 6.1 and 6.18
do), which carry its own decision, so the bits are kept or cleared
exactly as on a local file system. ENOSYS would
instead make the guest stop offloading copies for good. Copies to
other files are offloaded as before, and should the bits appear after
the check, the copy still clears them.

fallocate has no such fallback, so it still clears the bits for every
caller.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/passthrough/mod.rs | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/passthrough/mod.rs b/src/passthrough/mod.rs
index 29edd62..5d132d2 100644
--- a/src/passthrough/mod.rs
+++ b/src/passthrough/mod.rs
@@ -2665,6 +2665,25 @@ impl FileSystem for PassthroughFs {
         // Take just a read lock as we're not going to alter the file descriptor offset.
         let fd_out = data_out.file.get()?.read().unwrap().as_raw_fd();
 
+        // With KILLPRIV_V2, where the destination has setuid or setgid bits that a copy could
+        // clear, decline the copy with EOPNOTSUPP: the guest kernel then copies with ordinary
+        // writes, which carry its own decision whether to clear them (FUSE_WRITE_KILL_SUIDGID),
+        // made from the caller's capabilities, so the bits are kept or cleared as on a local file
+        // system. (Not ENOSYS, after which the guest would stop offloading copies altogether.)
+        // Should the bits appear after this check, the copy below still clears them.
+        if self.cfg.killpriv_v2 {
+            let mut st = MaybeUninit::<libc::stat64>::zeroed();
+            // Safe because this only writes to `st` and we check the return value.
+            if unsafe { libc::fstat64(fd_out, st.as_mut_ptr()) } == 0 {
+                // Safe because fstat64() succeeded.
+                let mode = unsafe { st.assume_init() }.st_mode;
+                let sgid_exec = libc::S_ISGID | libc::S_IXGRP;
+                if mode & libc::S_ISUID != 0 || mode & sgid_exec == sgid_exec {
+                    return Err(io::Error::from_raw_os_error(libc::EOPNOTSUPP));
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
         // There is no kill-suidgid flag for this operation. The guest kernel
         // cannot instruct us to strip privilege bits. Drop CAP_FSETID
         // unconditionally so the host kernel does it. This removes the bits
